400 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 171 0 R
/E (Automated extraction of characters using NLP tools became more prominent in the 2010s. Elson, Dames and McKeown [2] were the first to use quote detection and attribution for character extraction)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
401 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 171 0 R
/E (to build social networks from literature fiction. Characters are automatically extracted not just as string matches but as characters quoting conversation and therefore creating more semantic networks; however, it does depend on quote attribution, a difficult problem itself and one especially difficult with indirect speech or interior monologue in novels.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
402 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 171 0 R
/E (Bamman, Underwood, and Smith [3] trained Bayesian models on this existing annotation scheme and to predict a character type \(villain, hero, mentor, etc\) based on how character types co-occur with one another and the words that describe them. They found that the context in which a character appears \(the language describing the verb, noun or adjective that the character performs with\) is sufficient to statistically group the characters based on their role in the story. This also informs Lit Mind's relationship classification module.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
403 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 171 0 R
/E (The earliest attempt at computational literary analysis is by Franco Moretti [1], who described how one could approach the literature through a form of "distant reading" - analyzing a whole library of books by using statistical and graphical tools, rather than simply reading each page. In his book of 2005, Graphs, Maps, Trees, Moretti built interaction networks of characters of some 19th-century books by hand, and demonstrated how one might infer different genre, narrative style and era from them. He was more interested in the ideas and the abstraction, so the networks were built by hand, but that earlier work paved the way to the digital labor we now put into Lit Mind.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
