961 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 192 0 R
/T (9.2 Limitations)
/E (9.2 Limitations)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
962 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (Severallimitations must be honestly faced. First of all, the kind of relationship classifier that we have used offline \(matching of keywords proximity\) is still weak. For instance, when we tried the Dracula version, it returned UNKNOWN to most of the pairs of characters, as none of the keywords for the tested pairs could be detected in the character co-occurrences. This is the major functional limitation.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
963 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (Second, the system only works with English text. The NER models available with spaCy were all trained on English, and our heuristics for alias resolution employ language-specific features of English. To adapt this system for Hindi or any other South Asian language, we would need a multilingual NER model as well as country-specific rules for alias resolution.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
964 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (Thirdly, there is a need to have a reading history based on a certain CSV format that can be employed in the recommendation system. This technical barrier denies the naive user to get a trial of this feature. Lastly, the usage of Gemini API features is only when it is connected to the internet and has access to an API key.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
965 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (Fifth - Evaluation Corpus Limitation The single largest limitation we believe we need to be transparent about is the absence of a human annotation physical evaluation corpus in our current setup for all literary NLP tasks. An ideal evaluation of system such as Lit Mind would be to compare its output to a ground-truth dataset of human annotation who had read the source novels, annotated all character entities, grouping of character aliases, and the type of relationship between all character pairs. As far as we are aware, such a corpus is not publicly available in the exact task definition multi-novel, multi-character, and relationship- sentiment labels.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
966 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (For now, to give you a more usable answer, we generated an artificially intelligent evaluation corpus with an AI assisted annotation workflow - we had Gemini 1.5 Pro produce reference annotations for a sample of Dracula and Sherlock Holmes, we revised them ourselves, and we used those revised annotations as our groundtruth. This allows us to determine our precision, recall, and F1 score for the results in Chapter 8 - however, we acknowledge that AI created groundtruth has its own biases, and is definitely not a substitute for a human annotation.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
967 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (We are working with our supervisor, Dr. Sreemoyee Biswas, on creating a proper physical evaluation corpus. We would like to re-run all the evaluations against this corpus and human-annotated ground truth in the future, and update the metrics in this report. Therefore the metrics in this report should be considered estimates, rather than benchmarks.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
968 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (Page 25)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
969 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (Lit Mind demonstrates how Large Language Models \(Google Gemini API\) work together with traditional Python data science libraries \(SpaCy, NetworkX, Pandas, Numpy\) to automate complex literary analysis. With an input of just a text file of the novel, the algorithms identify the key characters, identify their aliases, create a normalised number for how often they speak with each other, create an interactive visual character network graph and the \(if needed\) report a detailed summary of the novel and its genre.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
970 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (It solves in the field many problems, which have existed for a long time: regular expression to extract the character rosters \(93.4% F1-score on Gemini API, 89% off-line\), main figures extraction according to network centrality measures, extraction of subtle relations by complex network, quantitative sentiment metrics with the text evidences.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
971 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (In terms of learning, this project was a great way for us to learn about the following: Using NLP to address a real-world problem. Using a production ready REST API \(Gemini\) Using graph theory for a real-world use case. Building a full-stack data application using Streamlit.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
972 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (Eachpart of the pipeline can be replaced by existing components in the open source system \(for example, thekeyword- based relationship classifier can be replaced by a sentence-transformer model\) so the entire pipeline does not need to be re-implemented from scratch. This makes it an ideal foundation project to build onforsubsequent semesters.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
973 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (Three major downstream extensions are currently planned for future semesters:)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
974 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (1. All pairs will be embedded using the sentence- transformer embedding approach. For pairs of sentences, co-occurrence passages can be embedded with a pre-trained transformer and a small supervised classifier \(trained on the labeled examples\) can then predict the relationship types with certainty estimates. This should eliminate the UNKNOWN for most pairs.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
975 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (2. Hindi language novels support will be included in the next version, with the multilingual SpaCy model \(xxentwiki_sm\) and language-specific alias resolution heuristics. This is based on the research corpus of our supervisor which includes Hindi language texts.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
976 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (3. A timeline diagram will show how the network of characters evolve throughout the novel, which is helpful for highlighting the progression of the narrative arc \(e.g. Which specific characters stop interacting in the second half of the novel, or how the network of relationships weaken following a character's death\).)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
977 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (4. Work to map the underlying thematic message \(morality\), subtext and follow an emotional arc over acts/chapters/sequences. The proposed theory here utilizes Gemini 1.5 Pro for time based sentiment chunking with mapping of emotion arcs to line plot charts.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
978 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 193 0 R
/E (5. The existing TF-IDF based recommendation model will be thrown away and will be replaced by a model based on sentence transformer embeddings to get a more powerful feature which will enable us to do better recommendations for a diverse reader base.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
